00:03:01.772 --> 00:03:02.980
MARK MANDEL: Oh, interesting.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.920
DAN SKAGGS: With some folks from
consulting and the industry,

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:07.450
as well as people--

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:10.900
a lady from National Institutes
of Health and the Google Health

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:11.920
representative.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.560
That was literally inspiring
and energizing hour from--

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.620
I'm a big advocate of people
having access to their own data

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:22.090
and owning their own data.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:24.820
And when you look at it
from the health care side,

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:28.720
I would love nothing more than
to have all of my health data

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.430
where I can get my hands on it.

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:32.110
And if I go to a
new provider, then I

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:37.178
can say, OK, you get access to
this for the next five days.

00:03:37.178 --> 00:03:38.470
MARK MANDEL: Here's my USB key.

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:40.550
DAN SKAGGS: Right, or I push
a button and the cloud says,

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:41.900
send it to his system, right?

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.190
It's even easier.

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:48.460
So that angle of it appeals to
me as a data geek, so to speak.

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:53.470
But then also the ability for
me to voluntarily push that

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:58.870
into big data to aid
in studies and research

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:00.910
