keep abstracting upwards

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:14.630
until you're not talking
about anything in specific?

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:17.480
Or do you start
breaking out into more--

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:20.270
a keynote for data scientists,
and a keynote for developers,

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:21.545
and a keynote for operators?

00:08:21.545 --> 00:08:24.170
AJA HAMMERLY: Well, we only have
one keynote stage, which makes

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:25.190
lots of things a challenge.

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:26.565
We have spotlight
sessions, which

00:08:26.565 --> 00:08:29.270
are kind of like more
tailored keynote type things.

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:32.030
But I mean, the temptation
is there to abstract.

00:08:32.030 --> 00:08:34.610
But as our lovely
podcast guests know,

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:37.400
when we have people who don't
talk about anything concrete,

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:39.059
it can get a little boring.

00:08:39.059 --> 00:08:41.150
So I don't know if we
figured it out yet.

00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:42.350
LAUREN BUCHMAN: Yeah,
I think you kind of

00:08:42.350 --> 00:08:44.330
have to just say we're
going to talk about one

00:08:44.330 --> 00:08:45.745
or two things in depth.

00:08:45.745 --> 00:08:47.120
And not everybody
